= Proving BNF Ambiguity

A grammar is ambiguous if at least one string in its language has two distinct derivations.

== Grammar 1

#grammar[
  #raw(block: true, lang: "text", "S -> X | Y\nX -> xXy | xy\nY -> xyY | ε")
]

Consider the single string `xy`. It can be generated in two different ways:

#grid(
  columns: (1fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 18pt,
  [*Derivation 1* #raw(block: true, lang: "text", "S -> X -> xy")],
  [*Derivation 2* #raw(block: true, lang: "text", "S -> Y -> xyY -> xy")],
)

Because the same terminal string `xy` has two distinct derivations, the grammar is ambiguous.

== Grammar 2

#grammar[
  #raw(block: true, lang: "text", "<S> -> <A>a\n<S> -> a<A>\n<A> -> a\n<A> -> ε")
]

Consider the single string `aa`. It can also be generated in two different ways:

#grid(
  columns: (1fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 18pt,
  [*Derivation 1* #raw(block: true, lang: "text", "<S> -> <A>a -> aa")],
  [*Derivation 2* #raw(block: true, lang: "text", "<S> -> a<A> -> aa")],
)

Since the terminal string `aa` has two distinct derivations, this grammar is ambiguous as well.
